-36, 44.\n3. Hex escape too short: `(\"\\\\x\", \"x\")` \u2192 raises ValueError \"invalid hex string escape ('\\x')\". Covers lines 31-34.\n4. Hex escape invalid: `(\"\\\\xzz\", \"xzz\")` \u2192 raises ValueError. Covers lines 35-38.\n5. Octal escape valid: `(\"\\\\101\", \"101\")` \u2192 returns `\"A\"`. Covers lines 39-41, 44.\n6. Octal escape invalid: `(\"\\\\9\", \"9\")` \u2192 raises ValueError \"invalid octal string escape ('\\9')\". Covers lines 42-43.\n\nWait, for the octal invalid case: `int(\"9\", 8)` raises ValueError, so that covers line 42-43. \n\nAlso, could test via `evalString` for integration, but the direct call is cleaner for coverage of all branches.\n\nLet me check the module name and imports. The file is `blib2to3/pgen2/literals.py`. So:\n\n```python\nfrom blib2to3.pgen2.literals import escape, simple_escapes\n```\n\nLet me verify with get_info tool. Let me call get_info on \"escape\" and \"simple_escapes\".\n\nActually, I should use the get_info tool to check the function signature and the simple_escapes dict. Let me do that.\n\nLet me write the test:\n\n```python\nimport pytest\n\nfrom blib2to3.pgen2.literals import escape, simple_escapes\n\n\nclass FakeMatch:\n    \"\"\"Minimal stand-in for re.Match returning fixed groups.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, whole, tail):\n        self._whole = whole\n        self._tail = tail\n\n    def group(self, *groups):\n        assert groups == (0, 1)\n        return (self._whole, self._tail)\n\n\ndef test_escape_simple():\n    for tail, expected in simple_escapes.items():\n        result = escape(FakeMatch(\"\\\\\" + tail, tail))\n        assert result == expected\n\n\ndef test_escape_hex_valid():\n    assert escape(FakeMatch(\"\\\\x41\", \"x41\")) == \"A\"\n    assert escape(FakeMatch(\"\\\\x7a\", \"x7a\")) == \"z\"\n\n\ndef test_escape_hex_too_short():\n    with pytest.raises(ValueError, match=\"invalid hex string escape\"):\n        escape(FakeMatch(\"\\\\x\", \"x\"))\n\n\ndef test_escape_hex_invalid():\n    with pytest.raises(ValueError, match=\"invalid hex string escape\"):\n        escape(FakeMatch(\"\\\\xzz\", \"xzz\"))\n\n\ndef test_escape_octal_valid():\n    assert escape(FakeMatch(\"\\\\101\", \"101\")) == \"A\"\n    assert escape(FakeMatch(\"\\\\0\", \"0\")) == \"\\x00\"\n\n\ndef test_escape_octal_invalid():\n    with pytest.raises(ValueError, match=\"invalid octal string escape\"):\n        escape(FakeMatch(\"\\\\9\", \"9\"))\n```\n\nWait, for the octal invalid case: `int(\"9\", 8)` raises ValueError. Good.\n\nHmm, but wait \u2014 for `test_escape_octal_valid` with `\"0\"`: `int(\"0\", 8)` = 0, `chr(0)` = `\"\\x00\"`. Good.\n\nAlso, should I test via `evalString` to make sure the integration works? The task says lines 26-44 don't execute when tested. The direct calls cover all lines. But maybe also add an integration test through `evalString` for the happy paths. Let me check what `evalString` looks like \u2014 I should use get_info.\n\nActually, let me also consider: the assert on line 27 `assert all.startswith(\"\\\\\")`. If we pass a fake match where all doesn't start with backslash, we'd get AssertionError. That's a branch too, but it's an assert \u2014 probably not needed for coverage, but could add a test. Actually, coverage counts lines, and line 27 executes in all tests. The branch `assert` failing might count as a partial branch in branch coverage. To be thorough, I could add a test that the assertion fires. But the task says \"execute all missing lines and branches\". Let me add a test for the assert failure too, to cover the failing branch of the assert.\n\nHmm, but is that desirable? It's testing defensive code. It does improve branch coverage. Let me include it.\n\nAlso, the branch `if esc is not None` \u2014 both true (simple escape) and false (hex/octal) paths are covered.\n\nLet me also double check `simple_escapes` content via get_info.\n\nAlso, should the tests use `monkeypatch`? There's no global state being modified here, so no cleanup needed. The FakeMatch is local. No state pollution.\n\nLet me call get_info for \"escape\", \"simple_escapes\", and \"evalString\" to confirm.\n\nLet me write the final test script:\n\n```python\nimport pytest\n\nfrom blib2to3.pgen2.literals import escape, evalString, simple_escapes\n\n\nclass FakeMatch:\n    \"\"\"Minimal re.Match stand-in returning fixed values for group(0, 1).\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, whole, tail):\n        self._whole = whole\n        self._tail = tail\n\n    def group(self, *groups):\n        assert groups == (0, 1)\n        return (self._whole, self._tail)\n\n\ndef test_escape_simple_escapes():\n    for tail, expected in simple_escapes.items():\n        assert escape(FakeMatch(\"\\\\\" + tail, tail)) == expected\n\n\ndef test_escape_hex_valid():\n    assert escape(FakeMatch(\"\\\\x41\", \"x41\")) == \"A\"\n    assert escape(FakeMatch(\"\\\\x7a\", \"x7a\")) == \"z\"\n    assert escape(FakeMatch(\"\\\\X41\", \"X41\")) == \"A\"  # uppercase X also startswith... \n```\n\nWait, `tail.startswith(\"x\")` \u2014 uppercase \"X\" doesn't start with lowercase \"x\". So `\"X41\"` would go to the octal branch, and `int(\"X41\", 8)` raises ValueError \u2192 \"invalid octal string escape\". So don't include that in hex test. Actually that could be an interesting extra test for the octal-invalid branch, but `\"\\\\9\"` already covers it.\n\nLet me also think about whether `escape` is importable directly. In blib2to3/pgen2/literals.py, `escape` is a module-level function, so yes.\n\nLet me verify with get_info first. I'll make parallel calls for \"escape\", \"simple_escapes\", \"evalString\".\n\nAfter confirming, write the tests.\n\nOne more consideration: the regex in evalString is `r\"\\\\(\\\\|'|\\\"|x[a-fA-F0-9]{2}|[0-7]{1,3})\"`. For an integration test through evalString:\n- `evalString(r\"'\\n'\")` \u2192 `\"\\n\"`\n- `evalString(r\"'\\x41'\")` \u2192 `\"A\"`\n- `evalString(r\"'\\101'\")` \u2192 `\"A\"`\n\nThese would cover lines 26-30, 31-32